### Asynchronous Database Reads and Writes

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/SwiftConcurrency.md

Demonstrates how to perform asynchronous read and write operations on a database writer using Swift Concurrency. This pattern is suitable for fetching data and modifying it safely.

```swift
let playerCount = try await writer.read {
    try Player.fetchCount(db)
}

let newPlayerCount = try await writer.write {
    try Player(name: "Arthur").insert(db)
    return try Player.fetchCount(db)
}
```

--------------------------------

### Fetching Players Asynchronously with `await`

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/SwiftConcurrency.md

An example of an asynchronous function that fetches players from the database using GRDB. The compiler correctly enforces the use of `await` for asynchronous database reads within an `async` function.

```swift
func fetchPlayers() async throws -> [Player] {
    try await writer.read(Player.fetchAll)
}
```

--------------------------------

### Asynchronous Database Writes with Swift Concurrency

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/Concurrency.md

Perform an asynchronous write operation using Swift's async/await. This method does not block the current thread and ensures transactions are rollbacked upon cancellation.

```swift
let newPlayerCount = try await dbQueue.write {
    try Player(name: "Arthur").insert($0)
    return try Player.fetchCount($0)
}
```

### DatabasePool > Concurrency

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/Extension/DatabasePool.md

A DatabasePool creates one writer SQLite connection and a pool of read-only SQLite connections. Unless configured as readonly, the database is set to WAL mode, enabling concurrent reads and writes. Write accesses are serialized through a single writer dispatch queue, ensuring only one thread writes at a time. Read accesses are distributed across multiple reader dispatch queues, generally allowing non-blocking operations unless the maximum number of concurrent reads is reached.

--------------------------------

### Differences between Database Queues and Pools

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/Concurrency.md

DatabasePool manages multiple database connections, allowing concurrent reads and writes using WAL mode. It serializes all writes but ensures reads are isolated, meaning they don't see changes from other threads. This allows for higher concurrency but requires careful consideration of potential read/write timing differences.

