### Asynchronous Database Reads and Writes

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/SwiftConcurrency.md

Demonstrates how to perform asynchronous read and write operations on a database writer using Swift Concurrency. This pattern is suitable for fetching data and modifying it safely.

```swift
let playerCount = try await writer.read {
    try Player.fetchCount(db)
}

let newPlayerCount = try await writer.write {
    try Player(name: "Arthur").insert(db)
    return try Player.fetchCount(db)
}
```

--------------------------------

### Asynchronous Database Writes with Swift Concurrency

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/Concurrency.md

Perform an asynchronous write operation using Swift's async/await. This method does not block the current thread and ensures transactions are rollbacked upon cancellation.

```swift
let newPlayerCount = try await dbQueue.write {
    try Player(name: "Arthur").insert($0)
    return try Player.fetchCount($0)
}
```

--------------------------------

### Asynchronous Database Reads with Swift Concurrency

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/Concurrency.md

Perform an asynchronous read operation using Swift's async/await. This method does not block the current thread and honors task cancellation.

```swift
let playerCount = try await dbQueue.read {
    try Player.fetchCount($0)
}
```

### Swift Concurrency and GRDB > Overview

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/SwiftConcurrency.md

GRDB leverages SQLite's concurrency features to ensure data-race safety with Swift 6. Database connections like DatabasePool allow for concurrent reads and writes, enabling background tasks to update data while the UI remains responsive. For application previews and tests, an in-memory DatabaseQueue is preferred. Both connection types offer the same database access methods for reading, writing, and observing changes, abstracting differences through the DatabaseWriter protocol while maintaining data integrity.

--------------------------------

### Non-Sendable Record Types

Source: https://github.com/groue/grdb.swift/blob/master/GRDB/Documentation.docc/SwiftConcurrency.md

In Swift 6 or with strict concurrency checking in Swift 5, the compiler flags non-Sendable types used in concurrent operations. By default, Swift classes are not Sendable and can cause issues with GRDB record classes. The recommended solution is to refactor record types from classes to structs composed of Sendable properties, as structs are inherently easier to make Sendable.

